---
title: Stovetop Mac and Cheese
description: One pot and three ingredients (elbow macaroni, evaporated milk, cheddar) make this mac and cheese.
tags: [pasta, one-pot]
course: main
icons: [cheese, pot-food, jug]
cuisine: American
difficulty: easy
source:
  name: Serious Eats
  author: J. Kenji López-Alt
  url: https://www.seriouseats.com/ingredient-stovetop-mac-and-cheese-recipe
servings: 2
time: 15m
why:
  - Boiling the macaroni in very little water keeps its starch in the pot, and that starch holds the cheese sauce together.
  - Evaporated milk has a lot of milk protein, which keeps melted cheese smooth instead of greasy.
  - Adding the milk after the pasta has cooked in water means nothing scorches on the bottom of the pan.
---

Put the @elbow macaroni{170%g}(6 oz) in a #medium saucepan{} and pour in just enough cold water to cover it. Add a pinch of @salt{} and bring to a boil over high heat, stirring often. Keep cooking and stirring until the water is nearly all absorbed and the macaroni is almost al dente, about ~boil{6%minutes}.

Right away, pour in the @evaporated milk{180%ml}(6 oz) and bring it to a boil. Add the @cheddar cheese{170%g}(6 oz, mild or medium, grated), turn the heat to low, and stir constantly until the cheese melts and the liquid cooks down to a creamy sauce, about ~stir{2%minutes}. Taste, add more salt if it needs it, and serve immediately.

> **Swaps** — Any good melting cheese works in place of cheddar, such as Fontina, Gruyère, or Jack.

> **Extras** — A dash of hot sauce, some mustard powder, or a small knob of butter can go in with the evaporated milk.

> **If it thickens** — If the sauce thickens while it sits, loosen it with a splash of water and a good stir.
